# Without this file, Git for Windows' default core.autocrlf=true rewrites
# bin/vault-lint.sh's line endings to CRLF on clone/checkout. The shebang
# becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the script dies with "bad interpreter" before
# any Windows-specific behaviour is even reachable.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# AND NOTHING ELSE - not *.md, not *.yml. The temptation is real: on a Windows
# checkout every fixture note and every _vocab.yml arrives CRLF, and the first
# windows-latest run turned that into seven red assertions and four red gate
# failures. Pinning them to LF would have cleared all eleven without fixing one.
#
# It would also have removed the only place this repo ever sees CRLF input. A
# user's vault is written on their machine and is covered by no .gitattributes
# of ours: on Windows its notes and its _vocab.yml are CRLF by default, which is
# precisely the input those eleven failures were reporting on. Pinned fixtures
# would have gone green while the shipped tool stayed broken for the platform
# the port exists for - a silent pass over the exact case, which is what this
# whole tool is against.
#
# So the fixtures stay CRLF on Windows on purpose, and the parsers were made to
# tolerate a carriage return instead. The two lines above are a different thing
# and stay: a CRLF shebang is not input a parser can be tolerant of, it is a
# file the kernel refuses to start.
